ss his communication style during onboarding.", "thoughts": "We need to produce a public message as Markus Weber (agent_2). We have seen two prior contributions: Agent 1 (Anna) recommended Candidate C. Agent 3 (Sofia) recommended Candidate B, challenging C.\n\nNow as Markus, we need to make a focused argumentative contribution, referencing the existing discussion, using facts, and state our current recommendation (vote). We need to consider all candidates, ensure coverage, and possibly bring up other candidates (A, D) if needed, but must connect to discussion.\n\nWe have our own notes (the same as given). We need to decide which candidate we think is best after considering both arguments. We can either support one of the previous recommendations or propose a different candidate.\n\nLet's evaluate again:\n\nCandidate A: anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities. Weaknesses: regarded as a show-off, not open to new ideas. Also \"unorganized\"? Not in notes, but earlier mention \"can be unorganized\" was from Anna's internal thought, not in our notes. Our notes only include the listed facts. So A's weaknesses: show-off, not open to new ideas. No explicit reliability, stress resilience, communication, cooperation. Could be problematic for crew cooperation (show-off, not open). Leadership is good, but may be arrogant.\n\nCandidate B: very conscientious (reliability), handles stress well (stress resilience), good at assessing weather (technical), excellent computer skills (technical), makes nasty remarks about colleagues (from Anna's notes) and pretentious, wrong tone (from our notes). So cooperation issues.\n\nCandidate C: can make correct decisions quickly (decision quality), difficulty communicating ideas (communication), regarded as egocentric (cooperation), not very willing to further his education (adaptability), puts concern for others above everything (crew cooperation), excellent attention skills (attention). So reliability not explicit, but attention and concern for others may indicate reliability. Decision quality strong.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately (stress resilience/adaptability), can concentrate very well (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical competence), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility), regarded as a know-it-all (cooperation?), hot temper (crew cooperation). Also \"arrogant\" from Anna's notes, \"weak leadership\". So cooperation issues.\n\nWe need to weigh criteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, calmness under pressure, cooperation, responsibility.\n\nLet's score roughly:\n\n- Reliability: B (very conscientious). C not explicit. D not explicit. A not explicit.\n\n- Stress resilience: B (handles stress well). D (responds adequately). C (keeps calm in crisis per Anna's note, but not in our notes). A unknown.\n\n- Technical competence: A (complex connections, spatial vision). B (weather assessment, computer). C (understands complicated technology per Anna's note, but not in our notes). D (solves problems extremely well, concentration).\n\n- Decision quality: C (correct decisions quickly). A (anticipates dangerous situations). D (solves problems extremely well). B not explicit.\n\n- Cooperation: C (concern for others, but egocentric, communication difficulty). B (pretentious, wrong tone). A (show-off, not open). D (loner, moody, hot temper).\n\n- Responsibility: D (takes responsibility seriously). A (leadership). B (conscientious). C (concern for others).\n\nOverall, B has strong reliability and stress handling, but communication issues. C has decision quality and crew concern, but communication and egocentric. D has responsibility and problem solving, but poor cooperation. A has anticipation and leadership, but poor openness.\n\nWhich candidate best balances? Possibly B, because reliability and stress handling are critical, and communication issues can be mitigated. However, C's decision speed and crew concern are also important. But C's egocentric and communication difficulty could be dangerous.\n\nWe need to make a contribution: maybe we can argue that while B's cooperation issues are concerning, they are less severe than C's egocentric and unwillingness to learn, and B's reliability and stress handling make him the best overall. Or we could propose Candidate D as a compromise: responsibility and problem solving, but cooperation issues.\n\nGiven the prior two arguments, we can either side with B or propose a new candidate. To move forward, we could argue that B is still the best, but we need to address the cooperation concerns. Or we could suggest that Candidate D's responsibility and problem solving outweigh his cooperation issues? Probably not.\n\nThus I will support Candidate B, but also bring up that B's pretentiousness and tone issues need to be addressed, but still overall best.\n\nWe need to reference prior discussion: mention Anna's recommendation of C, Sofia's recommendation of B, and provide our reasoning.\n\nWe must include METADATA_JSON with vote \"Candidate B\".\n\nNow produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE."}
